-- title:
--   Degree of a formal group isogeny is a power of p
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a natural number assumed prime, let $k$ be a field of characteristic $p$, and let $d$ be a natural number. Let $\Psi$ and $\Phi$ be $d$-dimensional formal group laws over $k$, that is, each is given by a $d$-tuple of power series in the $2d$ variables indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,d \oplus \mathrm{Fin}\,d$ with vanishing constant terms, linear part $X_i + Y_i$ in the $i$-th component, and satisfying the associativity identity under substitution; both are assumed commutative, i.e. invariant under interchanging the two blocks of variables. Let $\psi$ be a homomorphism from $\Psi$ to $\Phi$: a $d$-tuple $(\psi_i)$ of power series in $\mathrm{Fin}\,d$ variables over $k$ with zero constant terms such that $\psi_i(\Psi(X,Y)) = \Phi(\psi(X),\psi(Y))_i$ for every $i$. Assume that the quotient of the power series ring $k[[X_1,\dots,X_d]]$ by the ideal generated by the set of components $\psi_i$ is a finite $k$-module. The conclusion is that there exists a natural number $h$ with
--   $$\dim_k\; k[[X_1,\dots,X_d]]\big/(\psi_1,\dots,\psi_d) \;=\; p^{h}.$$
--
--   This is the statement that the order of the (infinitesimal) kernel of an isogeny of commutative formal group laws in characteristic $p$ is a power of $p$, the degree of the isogeny being measured by the $k$-dimension of the kernel algebra. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, where a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module is shown to have height four.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvFormalGroup_Hom_exists_finrank_quotient_span_range_eq_pow_of_finite.lean

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MvFormalGroup_BasicV2

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
